// facet_device/camera — which cameras this machine has.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE NEUTRAL BASE. `camera_linux.cplus` is the sibling the
// resolver picks on Linux; a target with no sibling lands here and answers an
// empty list, which is the same honesty `permissions.cplus` states at more
// length — a platform with no backend has no cameras it can name, and saying
// so beats inventing one.
//
// ---- what this is, and what it deliberately is not ----------------------------
//
// It ENUMERATES. It does not open a camera, negotiate a format, or deliver a
// frame — that is a streaming stack (PipeWire or V4L2 buffers on Linux,
// AVFoundation on Apple) and it is a much larger piece of work with a very
// different shape: a device list is a question with an answer, and a video
// stream is a lifetime.
//
// Enumeration on its own is worth having because it is what a settings screen
// needs — "which camera should I use", and "is there one at all" — and because
// it answers the question `permissions` cannot: the gate says who MAY, and this
// says what is actually plugged in.

import "stdlib/vec" as vec;
import "stdlib/text" as text;

// One capture device. `id` is what to hand back to a future open — a path on
// Linux — and `name` is the human one the device reports about itself, which is
// the only string a picker should show.
struct Camera {
    id: text::Text,
    name: text::Text,
}

fn supported() -> bool { return false; }

fn list() -> vec::Vec[Camera] { return vec::new::[Camera](); }

fn count() -> usize {
    let cams: vec::Vec[Camera] = list();
    return cams.count();
}
